WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:06.052
In July 1935 an American vice consul in Shanghai pinned a small photograph to a

2
00:00:06.052 --> 00:00:11.805
form, and an eighteen-year-old student signed his name across it: Ieoh-ming Pe.

3
00:00:12.155 --> 00:00:15.708
His father, Tsuyee Pei, was a manager at the Bank of China.

4
00:00:16.058 --> 00:00:19.835
He sailed on the steamship President Coolidge

5
00:00:19.835 --> 00:00:23.779
and arrived at San Francisco on 28 August 1935.

6
00:00:24.129 --> 00:00:27.364
He was admitted as a student under the Immigration Act of

7
00:00:27.364 --> 00:00:30.429
1924, which allowed in Chinese students at a time when

8
00:00:30.429 --> 00:00:34.345
almost all other Chinese immigration to the United States was banned.

9
00:00:34.695 --> 00:00:37.486
Because the consulate in Shanghai had spelled his

10
00:00:37.486 --> 00:00:40.733
name "Pe," that is the name on his early American papers.

11
00:00:41.083 --> 00:00:44.095
Pei started at the University of Pennsylvania but

12
00:00:44.095 --> 00:00:47.538
soon moved to the Massachusetts Institute of Technology.

13
00:00:47.888 --> 00:00:51.080
Then, in 1937, war broke out between China

14
00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:54.424
and Japan, followed by the Second World War.

15
00:00:54.774 --> 00:00:56.632
Going home became more and more difficult.

16
00:00:56.982 --> 00:01:01.370
By 1946 he was married to Eileen Loo and teaching at Harvard.

17
00:01:01.720 --> 00:01:04.843
Harvard asked for him to stay one more year as a teaching fellow.

18
00:01:05.193 --> 00:01:07.701
The immigration officers agreed, but noted:

19
00:01:08.301 --> 00:01:10.779
“It is understood that the subject expects to return

20
00:01:10.779 --> 00:01:13.305
to China to teach after this year's work at Harvard.”

21
00:01:13.905 --> 00:01:17.164
To change from a visiting student to a permanent resident, Pei

22
00:01:17.164 --> 00:01:20.685
and his wife had to leave the United States and apply from outside.

23
00:01:21.035 --> 00:01:24.621
They went to Canada, received immigrant visas at Saint

24
00:01:24.621 --> 00:01:28.207
John, New Brunswick, on 23 September 1947, and crossed

25
00:01:28.207 --> 00:01:32.390
back into the United States at Calais, Maine, three days later.

26
00:01:32.740 --> 00:01:36.246
Twelve years after he arrived, he was finally allowed to stay.

27
00:01:36.596 --> 00:01:39.136
His most famous design is the glass pyramid that

28
00:01:39.136 --> 00:01:41.623
forms the main entrance of the Louvre in Paris.

29
00:01:41.973 --> 00:01:44.285
What would make you stay in a country that

30
00:01:44.285 --> 00:01:46.872
kept telling you your visit was only temporary?
